    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short: **no, you should not make an issue of the fact that the panel was entirely men.** Practically and legally, it provides no leverage, and raising it would likely be viewed as misdirected or defensive. \n\nHere is an objective breakdown of what that panel composition actually signals, why the outcome likely happened, and how to handle the situation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Does the all-male panel signal discrimination against you?\n* **Between you and the successful candidate, gender is a neutral factor:** Because both you and the candidate who was hired are men, an all-male panel does not point to sex-based discrimination favoring one of you over the other.\n* **It reflects industry demographics, not necessarily a conspiracy:** Structural engineering—particularly at senior leadership and director levels—remains heavily male-dominated. While modern HR best practice encourages diverse interview panels, homogenous panels are still common simply because senior management in many consultancies is homogenous.\n* **Affinity bias may still exist, but not strictly along gender lines:** Homogenous panels are often susceptible to \"similar-to-me\" bias (informal rapport, shared communication styles, or personal connections). The panel may have clicked more easily with the other candidate on an informal level, but this is about personal chemistry and perceived culture fit rather than gender.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What the announcement actually reveals\nThe deciding factor is almost certainly highlighted in the firm's own announcement: **\"excellent client relationships.\"**\n\n* **Commercial vs. Technical focus:** At the \"Head of Group\" level in private consultancies, the role is often primarily about **business development, client retention, and bringing in fees**, with secondary emphasis on pure technical design. \n* **Experience trade-off:** You clearly have superior technical tenure (15 years vs. 8, master's degree, three major project leadership roles). However, consultancies frequently choose a candidate with strong commercial charm and established client ties over a technically stronger engineer, especially if the existing team already has solid technical execution capacity.\n* **Cost considerations:** A professional with 8 years of experience usually commands a lower salary package than a senior leader with 15+ years of experience.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The 2-week delay for written feedback\nA two-week silence after requesting written feedback is standard in corporate environments for a few reasons:\n* Written feedback carries legal liability, so HR often reviews or edits notes before anything is sent externally.\n* Interviewers are billable fee-earners who routinely deprioritize post-interview administration.\n* It is not inherently a sign of bad faith, but rather standard bureaucratic drag.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended next steps\n1. **Send a polite, professional follow-up:** \n   Keep it brief and forward-looking. Focus on constructive professional development rather than challenging their decision:\n   > *\"I understand the panel is busy, but I wanted to follow up on my request for brief feedback regarding the Head of Structural Engineering role. As I was very impressed by the team and remain keen on leadership opportunities, any specific insights on areas for my professional development would be greatly appreciated.\"*\n2. **Do not bring up the panel's gender composition:** It will distract from your legitimate qualifications and will not yield any productive outcome.\n3. **Assess cultural alignment:** If the firm values commercial schmoozing or lower-cost appointments over deep technical rigor and seasoned leadership, it may simply not have been an environment where your experience and approach would have been properly supported.\n",
